#------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Early Development Stage (1995-1999)
#------------------------------------------------------------------------------

msgid "changelog.early.1999.title"
msgstr "📜 Early Development Stage (1995-1999)"

msgid "changelog.early.1999.items"
msgstr ""
"<li><strong>1999 - Open Source Eve:</strong> Standardized architecture to adapt to different server environments, completing the final transformation from \"personal tool\" to \"standardized product\". Enhanced detection: Significantly expanded crawler recognition library; Fixed mode: Improved CGI script runtime mode; Community testing: Conducted testing among a small circle of peers, completed code cleanup and documentation drafting based on feedback.</li>"
"<li><strong>1998 - Functional Deepening:</strong> Refined data dimensions and improved processing efficiency, entering high-frequency internal private iterations. Asynchronous processing: Established the \"offline log parsing\" logic to ensure statistics don't occupy web server real-time resources; Dimension expansion: Added recognition capabilities for browser types, operating systems, and HTTP status codes; Code optimization: Leveraged Perl's cross-platform features to test compatibility in Unix/Linux and Windows environments.</li>"
"<li><strong>1997 - Project Inception:</strong> Officially recognized in documentation as the project's founding year, transitioning from \"scattered scripts\" to \"analysis engine\". Architecture definition: Established static report generation mode, first time outputting analysis results as HTML pages with graphical interface; Feature library building: Began establishing initial recognition rule libraries for search engines and robots; Environmental adaptation: Benefiting from the standardization of Common Log Format (CLF), scripts began to have generalization capabilities.</li>"
"<li><strong>1995-1996 - Concept Germination:</strong> Responding to the need for access.log analysis after the popularity of Apache HTTP Server (1995). No formal project name yet, mainly experimental code snippets by author Laurent Destailleur. Written in early Perl 5, implementing basic regex matching for counting total hits and simple file type filtering (e.g., distinguishing .html from .gif). Used only for internal traffic observation on the author's personal website (such as early chiensderace.com prototype).</li>"